From: PW3 Android port
Subject: [PW3] SystemUI: a notification ends somewhere the reader can see

With three notifications in the shade on V85, nothing marks where one ends and
the next begins. Measured off the screenshot, down one column:

  * every card is `#FFFFFF`, and so is the shade behind them;
  * the only thing between two cards is a single row of `#EEEEEE`;
  * the low priority row at the bottom is `#F5F5F5`, and the action row
    (「分享 删除」) inside the first card is `#EEEEEE`.

`#F5F5F5` is 10 of 255 from white and `#EEEEEE` is 17: one step of the sixteen
this panel has, which is why rule 1 of docs/design/eink-ui.md names those two
values and says they cannot be used to tell regions apart here.

What separates notifications in AOSP is not any of those -- it is the
elevation shadow each row casts on the one below. This SystemUI draws in
software (patch 0003), where no shadow is drawn at all: `FakeShadowView` only
sets `translationZ` and hands the renderer an outline, and the software
renderer has nothing to do with either. Rule 3 was written for exactly this --
「软件绘制根本不画 elevation 阴影，白色浮层落在白底上就没有边界」 -- and its
answer is a solid outline, the same one the dialogs, menus, toasts, task cards
and search boxes already carry.

The outline is drawn by `NotificationBackgroundView` rather than put in the
background drawable, because `setTint()` paints that drawable through a
`SRC_ATOP` colour filter: a media notification with a background colour of its
own, or a row being interpolated towards one, would take the outline with it
and the boundary would disappear again for exactly the row that has it least.
Drawn in `draw()`, after the background and on the same rectangle inset by half
its width, no tint can reach it. The drawable keeps the same
`pw3_corner_radius`, so the fill and the line follow the same curve, to within
the part of a pixel by which an inset arc of the same radius differs. The one
thing an outline outside the drawable does not get for free is the drawable's
own alpha, which `setDrawableAlpha()` sets -- `ActivatableNotificationView`
fades the normal background of a row that cannot be dimmed while the shade
dims -- so that setter hands the alpha to the outline as well; a black frame
around a fill that is no longer there would be worse than no frame.

Three values follow:

  * the space between rows was 0.5dp, one pixel, which would put two outlines
    together into a single thick line. 4dp of shade between them reads as the
    gap it is. `notification_divider_height` is that space, which
    `StackScrollAlgorithm` and `NotificationShelf` also use;
  * `notification_children_container_divider_height` followed it, and it is
    not a space -- it is a line, painted in `notification_divider_color`
    between the children of an expanded group. It keeps the height it had;
  * the low priority background becomes white. It was `#F5F5F5`, invisible,
    and white is also the value `ActivatableNotificationView
    .setBackgroundTintColor()` compares against to decide to apply no colour
    filter at all.

The `#EEEEEE` action row inside a card is in framework-res and is left alone
here; it is a tint within one card, not the boundary between two.

